    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_6e7d11183ade0e7084dd2eff4cebc2e2668127ff5ecce683f8ac5599c0e42014",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Uncertainty can be made into computational control flow: cheap evaluation handles routine cases, while doubtful signs, anomalous counts, or possible interval escape trigger a more expensive formula, greater precision, or a wider interval. The epistemic strength differs across the examples: the zeta methods use explicit error envelopes for sign decisions, whereas the Jensen computation reports anomaly-driven precision and interval checks without an exact forward-error certificate.",
