    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_8cf696eb9b5858cc44fa36579c65316851f5d513c1490c7fe4d51967d2590689",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "A very small consecutive zero gap becomes a de Bruijn-Newman witness only after normalization by an interaction sum over all other zeros. A broad computation may discover the candidate and a local high-precision window may estimate its sensitive neighborhood, but certified exterior bounds and the Lehmer-pair criterion are what convert it into a one-sided lower bound for Lambda. Later sensitivity analysis shows that, once the normalized product is small, the squared gap supplies the leading contribution while the interaction remains logically indispensable.",
